Date: Sun, 24 Feb 2013 22:11:03 +0000
Subject: Re: [stan-dev] Re: Thinking out loud about missing data and convergence
Message-ID: <cWA5b7BiV1A.y5X4jPreiwIJ@stan-dev.groups.google.com>
In-Reply-To: <cWA5b7BiV1A.teL35fD0Kd0J@stan-dev.groups.google.com>
References: <cWA5b7BiV1A.qJUQEnfLWM4J@stan-dev.groups.google.com> <cWA5b7BiV1A.teL35fD0Kd0J@stan-dev.groups.google.com>
Archived-At: <https://groups.google.com/g/stan-dev/c/cWA5b7BiV1A/m/y5X4jPreiwIJ>
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset="utf-8"
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

I'd think in high dimensions, it'd be terrible, because
no points are close to each other on average, so kNN tends
to break down.   At that point, what I see people in the
machine learning literature do is try to find submanifolds
of lower dimension on which the relevant values lie.

But we're not really doing multivariate convergence analysis,
just a bunch of marginals.  (I believe Brooks and Gelman extended
R-hat to a multivariate setting.)

Stan's lp__ should be OK up to a proportion -- I wonder how
much of a problem we get by introducing an arbitrary rescaling?

- Bob

On 2/24/13 4:12 AM, Ben Goodrich wrote:
> On Sunday, February 24, 2013 3:52:21 AM UTC-5, Bob Carpenter wrote:
>
>     Is the idea that the difference should go to zero
>     in the limit as you get better non-parametric density
>     estimates?
>
>
> I think it would be zero asymptotically if Stan's lp__ reflected the normalizing constants. So, the difference will be
> non-zero but shouldn't be changing much as the number of draws from the stationary distribution increases.
>
>     How do you monitor convergence of the density
>     estimator?
>
>
> I don't think anyone has derived great theoretical results. You can draw a finite number of times from a known joint
> distribution like multivariate normal and then see how long it takes for the non-parametric estimator to get close.
>
> Ben
